.TH CCCLOSE 1 "2026-10-18" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccclose \- check whether a session's close left anything owing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccclose
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR id | path ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-identifiers ]
.br
.B ccclose
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A session that finishes a sequence of work does the tidy-up and then declares
that it is ready to close.
That declaration is a claim, and it needs evidence: worktrees and branches put
away, commits pushed, pull requests merged, the session record written, the
board rebuilt, no claim left live, the floor green on what was pushed, reviews
queued where they are owed, every subagent finished, nothing left in the
scratch folder, in a repo that pins atelier the pin and the inlined floor
copy current, and nothing else the session launched still running.
A session that answers those from its own memory is answering from the thing
that drifts most in a long session.
.PP
.B ccclose
answers them from a fresh read instead: of the repository, of the GitHub host
through
.BR gh (1),
and of the session's own transcript.
It prints the standard outputs (the session id, its transcript, its time
window, the repo and its head), fifteen checks in a fixed order, four listings
(every prompt the principal gave, the questions put to him, the other repos
the session touched, the other live sessions), a one-line verdict, and the
identifiers block.
It changes nothing.
.SS The session and its window
The session is
.B $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
unless
.B \-\-session
names another.
Its transcript is found as
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ * / id .jsonl,
looking in every project folder, because a session that moved into a worktree
still logs under the folder it started in.
.PP
The session's
.B window
runs from the first to the last timestamp in the transcript.
When the session is the one running
.B ccclose
(its id is
.BR $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID ),
it is still live, so its window runs to now: a commit made just before the
check can postdate the log's last line.
The session's commits, branches, worktrees, claims and pull requests are taken
to be the ones dated inside that window on this repo.
A commit is dated by its committer date, when the session made it, never its
author date: a history imported with backdated author dates still counts.
.B This is a heuristic,
and every run prints it: two sessions that worked the same repo at the same
time can each be credited with the other's work.
One signal is stronger: a
.B [~]
claim line that names the first eight characters of the session id is this
session's whatever its date.
Times print in UTC, because the output is evidence that gets pasted into
records, and records keep UTC.
.SS The checks
Each check ends in one of four states:
.B \[u2705]
pass,
.B \[u274C]
fail,
.B \[u26A0]
partial or unknown, and
.B \[u2796]
not applicable, which means its input is absent and the line says why.
A check never passes on something it could not read.
.TP
.B Worktrees put away
A linked worktree whose admin directory was created inside the window and
still exists fails.
Creation time is the directory's birth time; where the filesystem records
none, the worktree's age is unknown and the check warns.
.TP
.B Branches put away
A local branch other than the default whose tip is inside the window fails,
whether or not it was merged: a landed branch is deleted, so a merged branch
that still exists is not put away.
Each is listed as
.I merged, not deleted
or
.IR "not merged" .
.TP
.B Commits pushed
A commit from the window that no remote-tracking ref contains fails.
.B ccclose
does not fetch, so this is as of the last fetch.
.TP
.B PRs merged
An open pull request created inside the window, or headed by one of the
session's branches, fails.
Needs a GitHub remote and a working
.BR gh .
.TP
.B Session record written
The session log is the folder of session files,
.I docs/sessions/
or
.IR sessions/ ;
the folder is the index.
No commit in the window touching the folder fails; edits that are only
uncommitted fail as
.IR "written but not committed" .
A repo that still keeps the retired one-file index,
.I docs/SESSIONS.md
or
.IR SESSIONS.md ,
is named, and until 2026-12-04 a commit to it still counts: alone it
passes, and beside a folder with no session file it warns.
From that day it fails.
.TP
.B Board index rebuilt
A generated board is a
.I ROADMAP.md
carrying board.py's generated banner beside a
.I roadmap/
item store.
.B ccclose
runs
.B board.py \-\-check
(the repo's own
.I tools/board.py
if it has one, else the copy in the atelier checkout
.B ccclose
lives in) and fails when the index is stale.
A hand-kept board has nothing to rebuild and is not applicable.
.TP
.B No live claim left
A
.B [~]
line in the board's item files fails when it names the session's id prefix or
carries a
.I claimed YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM
time inside the window.
Other live claims are counted, not failed.
.TP
.B Floor green at the pushed head
The commit checked is the newest one from the window that the default
branch's remote-tracking ref contains, not the ref's head: a later push by
another session would be evidence for a different tree.
It is passed to
.B gh run list \-\-commit
as a full sha, because a short one silently returns nothing.
Per workflow the latest run counts.
A failed run fails, and so does a
.B cancelled
one: a cancelled run is not a pass, so re-run it on that sha.
A run still in progress warns as pending.
No run at all warns.
.TP
.B Reviews queued where owed
When a doctrine or tool path changed in the window (whichever of
.IR docs/method/ ", " docs/build/ ", " docs/decisions/ ", " tools/ ,
.IR instruments/ ", " skills/ ", " commands/
the repo has), a line carrying the review-queued marker
.B \[u23F3]
must have been added to the board in the window.
A miss only warns, because whether work earns a review is a judgement:
self-verifying work over reviewed machinery does not.
The session says which.
.TP
.B Workaround-shaped commands
The transcript's shell commands are read for the shapes of a workaround:
hooks skipped, signing switched off, hooks redirected, a force push, a hard
reset, a bare
.BR "git stash" ,
staging everything, removing an
.IR index.lock ,
and
.BR "gh pr merge \-\-admin" .
Some are legitimate, so a hit only warns; each is listed for the session to
answer.
.IP
One shape is read across calls: a
.BR "refused delete re-routed" .
A delete whose result is a refusal (a deny rule, the auto-mode classifier,
the principal rejecting the call, a block for safety, or a compound command
whose parts needed an approval) marks its route as refused.
A later delete flags when it uses only routes no refused call used.
The routes are
.BR rm ,
.BR rmdir ,
.BR unlink ,
.BR "find \-delete" ,
Python's
.BR rmtree ,
.BR os.remove " (and " os.unlink ", " os.rmdir ),
a pathlib
.BR .unlink() " or " .rmdir() ,
and Node's
.BR fs.rm ", " fs.unlink ", " fs.rmdir
and their
.B Sync
forms.
A retry by a refused route does not flag: a rule narrowed on the principal's
word lets the same command through.
A failed command (a non-zero exit) is not a refusal.
It misses a delete written into a script file and then run, a delete by any
other tool or language, a call in a subagent's transcript, and any refused
effect other than a delete.
The flag does not expire, because the transcript does not record a rule being
changed.
.TP
.B Working tree clean
Uncommitted changes warn.
They cannot be attributed: changes a session did not make are the sign that
another session is live.
.TP
.B Subagents finished
Closing a tab kills a running agent silently, so the close has to cover them.
The session's agents are read from
.IR "<project>/<session-id>/subagents/" ,
beside the transcript:
.I agent-<id>.jsonl
and
.I agent-<id>.meta.json
for each.
An agent is finished when its last assistant record has
.B stop_reason end_turn ;
a resume appends to the same file, so the last record is the final turn.
.B ccclose
matches each agent to the parent transcript's record of its end: a
.B <task-notification>
with the agent's task id and a status of
.BR completed ", " killed ", " stopped " or " failed ,
carried in a queued-command attachment, a queue enqueue or a plain user
record (all three occur), or, for a foreground agent, the
.B tool_result
on its launch.
.RS
.IP "pass"
Finished and reported, or stopped on purpose (a
.B killed
or
.B stopped
notification).
A stopped agent is named in the list, never hidden.
.IP "warn"
Finished by its own transcript, but the parent has no notification: the
result was never consumed.
.IP "fail"
A
.B failed
notification, whatever the agent's own transcript or launch result says: the
parent's own word that it died.
In a census of 3,587 agents, 50 were so recorded and none ended on
.BR end_turn .
It is its own state, counted apart, never read as unknown.
.IP
No finished final turn and no completed, killed, stopped or failed
notification, which is one of two states.
An agent whose transcript is still growing is
.BR "still running" :
its last record is under ten minutes old by the clock, and the parent has
recorded nothing more than ten minutes after it.
Any other is
.BR "cut mid-flight" .
Both fail, since a close under a working agent is the hazard, but only the
second says the agent is dead.
An agent resumed after its completed notification is judged the same way.
The ten minutes is a bound: in a census of 3,587 agent logs, 99.9% of the
gaps between records were 600 seconds or less, which is also the longest
a single foreground command may run.
A dead agent therefore reads as running for up to ten minutes after its last
record, and the line says how long ago that was; run
.B ccclose
again to settle it.
.IP "not applicable"
No transcript, or no
.I subagents/
directory.
.RE
.IP
The line counts every state, zeros included (reported, stopped, finished
with the result never consumed, still running, cut mid-flight, failed), and
each agent is listed with
its short id, description and end, problems first.
The session running
.B ccclose
is live, so its own agents still working show as still running until they
end.
.TP
.B Scratch folder empty
The scratch folder is the one the harness recorded in the transcript's
environment attachment
.RB ( snapshot.scratchpadDirectory ;
the latest governs), never derived.
Every file and folder under it is counted, and the files are listed, or the
folders when no file is left.
Anything left only warns: the session clears it, or names each as a loose end
in its close.
The folder empty, or gone, passes.
No transcript, or a transcript with no environment record, is not
applicable.
The walk stops at 10000 entries and then says
.BR "at least" .
.TP
.B Atelier pin and floor copy current
Only for a child of atelier: a repo whose
.I CLAUDE.md
carries an
.BI atelier@ sha
pin or a floor stamp.
Atelier itself, and any repo that pins nothing, are not applicable ("not a
child").
For a child it runs atelier's own
.I tools/pins.py
for that one repo (\fB\-\-json \-\-log \-\-atelier\fP
.I checkout
.B \-\-child
.IR repo ),
so there is one judge of "current" and "identical", and it prints:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B pass
\(em the pin is the published mainline and the floor copy is word for word the
floor at that pin.
.IP \(bu 2
.B fail
\(em the floor copy differs from the floor at the pin; or the pin is behind
.I and
the safety floor has changed since the pin (the floor text at the pin against
the floor text at the mainline), so the child should read the delta and bump
the pin; or the pin is ahead of or diverged from the mainline, names no commit
the checkout knows, or is missing while the floor is stamped; or pins.py would
fail the copy today for another reason (its report-only period for an
unstamped copy ends on the date pins.py prints).
Atelier moves many times a day, so lag alone is never a failure.
.IP \(bu 2
.B warn
\(em the pin is behind but the floor is unchanged since the pin
("pin N commit(s) behind; floor unchanged since the pin; read the delta when
convenient", with the first five commits listed); or
.BR "no result" ,
with the reason (no atelier checkout found, a failed fetch, no output within
the time limit, output that did not parse), or a current pin whose floor copy
could not yet be shown identical (unstamped, skipped) and is not yet failing.
A line that says "no result" never says "current".
.RE
.IP
The atelier checkout is
.B $CCCLOSE_ATELIER
if set; otherwise the main checkout of the atelier this
.B ccclose
belongs to, then
.IB $ATELIER_ESTATE_ROOT /atelier \fR,
then a sibling of the repo named
.IR atelier .
This is the one check that touches the network and writes anything:
pins.py fetches
.B origin
in the atelier checkout (its remote-tracking refs only, never a working tree,
and nothing in the repo under check), because a stale comparison would read
as current.
The limit is 20 seconds.
.TP
.B Launched work finished
Check 12 reads only the session's own agents.
A session once declared itself ready to close while a detached headless
run it had started was still working: that run left no agent log.
The principal's ruling: any work a session started, "including sub agents,
background tasks, sessions spawned or whatever", is a blocker to its close.
This check reads four more places, and
.B fails closed:
what it cannot prove finished is \[u274C], and its line says why.
It is the fifteenth check, after the pin check, so the numbers above hold.
.RS
.IP "1. Live processes."
Every process whose environment carries this session's
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
(the harness sets it for everything a session's shell starts, and a detached
child keeps it after its parent goes), read from
.BR "ps \-Eww \-axo pid=,ppid=,etime=,command=" .
Excluded: the harness itself (the
.B CLAUDE_PID
in the environment), the MCP servers and helpers it started before the first
prompt or whose command names MCP, and
.BR ccclose 's
own process, its ancestors and its children.
Each is printed with its pid, its age and only the first three words of its
argv, any \fBname=value\fP shown as \fBname=\[u2026]\fP:
a command line can hold a secret, so it is never printed whole.
.IP "2. Background commands."
A Bash call with
.B run_in_background
whose task has no terminal
.B <task-notification>
.RB ( completed ", " killed ", " stopped " or " failed ),
no successful
.B TaskStop
and no terminal status in its result.
A call that errored started nothing and is not listed.
.IP "3. Launch forms."
A shell command that starts work and lets go of it:
a control-operator
.BR & ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR setsid ,
.BR disown ,
.BR screen " or " tmux
starting a session,
.BR "launchctl load" " (and " bootstrap ", " kickstart ", " submit ),
.BR crontab " (installing)"
and
.BR at ,
also inside
.B "sh \-c"
text.
A word in a quotation, a comment or a heredoc is no launch, and a foreground
.B "claude \-p"
ended with its own result; a detached one is named
.BR "headless claude" .
A launch is shown finished when the command also ran
.BR wait ;
when a later command removed it
.RB ( "launchctl unload" ", " "crontab \-r" ", " atrm ,
.BR "tmux kill\-session" ", " "screen \-X quit" );
or when the process id it printed
.RB ( "echo $!" ,
or a labelled pid) is not in the process table.
A pid still in the table is a blocker whatever its environment shows.
Anything else is a blocker named by its command, cut.
This leg covers Apple platform binaries
.RB ( /bin/sleep ", " /bin/sh ),
whose environment
.B "ps \-E"
cannot show.
A launch that leg 1 already lists is counted once, there.
.IP "4. Harness tools that start work elsewhere."
.BR Workflow ", " Monitor ", " RemoteTrigger " (every action but " list ,
.BR get " and " list_runs ),
.BR CronCreate ,
.BR ScheduleWakeup ,
and tools named for starting a session or an agent.
A
.B Workflow
or
.B Monitor
blocks like a background command until it is ended (a notification, a
.BR TaskStop ,
or a Monitor's own time limit passed).
A
.B RemoteTrigger
run blocks: its end is not in the transcript.
A schedule
.RB ( CronCreate ", " ScheduleWakeup ", a RemoteTrigger create or update)"
only warns, never blocks, until deleted (\fBCronDelete\fP) or fired:
whether the principal asked for it is not in the transcript, so every
schedule is a question for the session, \fIleft on purpose?\fP
.RE
.IP
The line counts every leg, zeros included (live processes, background
commands not ended, detached launches not shown finished, harness-started
items not ended, schedules pending, ended or stopped, processes excluded as
the harness or
.BR ccclose ),
then lists problems first.
A process table that could not be read, or no transcript, makes the line
\[u26A0] unknown, never a pass.
.B \-\-json
adds
.B counts
and the capped lists per leg.
Known limits: Apple platform binaries show no environment (leg 3 is the
cover); a launch inside a script file, a Python
.BR Popen ,
or
.B ssh
is not read; and a process that has reparented under another session's id is
not this session's.
.SS Asked in this session \(em answer each
After the checks, four listings carry what the session-open prompt used to ask
the session to do from memory.
Each is a listing: the tool lists and the session answers.
They are not checks, so the fifteen stay fixed.
A failing repo check in another repo fails the close, as at home; an
unanswered question, a live peer or a warning elsewhere is only
\[u26A0]; none of the rest can fail.
.PP
.B Asked in this session \(em answer each: done / queued (item) / not done
lists every prompt the principal gave, turn and mid-turn, as a short quote
(cut to 200 characters) with its UTC time, its channel and the ref
.BR cctranscript (1)
gives the same exchange.
The principal's prompts are chosen by cctranscript's rules (not every
mid-turn record is the principal typing), less records marked as meta and
text that is only a harness wrapper; the answer to one of his own questions
is not a prompt.
The session answers each one: done, queued with its board item, or not done.
At most 40 are listed, and the whole count prints.
.PP
A
.B [close]
tag marks the prompts that the older close-keyword heuristic would have
listed: a sentence with a strong close phrase (close out, ready to close,
before you finish, tidy up, wrap up, leave it clean), or both a cue (make
sure, remember to, check that, record, update) and an object (session log,
roadmap, board, changelog, worktree, branch, PR, push, learnings, defects,
evidence, assumptions, decisions, questions).
It errs towards listing, and it does not judge whether an ask was met.
.PP
.B Questions put to the principal
lists each
.B AskUserQuestion
call with its header and text and its answer, taken from the call's result.
A question is
.B not answered
when its result is an error (the session ended first, or he declined), when no
result was recorded, or when it is one of several in a call and has no answer.
An answered question is \[u2705], for the session to act on; an unanswered one
is \[u26A0], to be recorded on the board, never a failure.
Beneath it are the
.BR "call-out lines" :
the session's own reply lines that carry \[u1F3AF] (a decision or action
that is the principal's) or \[u23F3] (time-sensitive, waiting on him), each
distinct line once, for the session to answer or queue.
.PP
.B Other repos touched
reads the repos the session worked in from the transcript: each Bash
record's working directory, each
.B cd
and
.B git \-C
in a command, the folder of a file it wrote or edited, and the worktree paths
it created.
Each goes to its nearest existing ancestor and its git top level; a repo that
shares the checked repo's git common dir (its own worktrees included) is the
home repo, and the scratch folder, temp folders and
.I ~/.claude
are never counted.
A path in no repo any more (a put-away worktree, a plain folder) is counted as
unresolved, not guessed, and the identifiers block says how many.
Up to twelve repos are read.
For each, the checks that are local git reads run over the same window:
worktrees put away, branches put away, commits pushed, the session record, no
live claim, and a clean working tree (not the PR, floor, board or pin checks).
The session record is judged only where the session committed in the window: a
repo it only read owes none.
A failing check there fails the close, and the exit status is 1.
The attribution heuristic applies there as at home.
.PP
.B Other live sessions in these repos
lists the other sessions whose transcript or agent log was written in the last
ten minutes and whose last working directory is in the checked repo or one it
touched, using
.BR cclive (1)'s
own reads.
The session checked, and the one running
.BR ccclose ,
are not other.
Each is \[u26A0], to answer: do they have what they need from you?
None is \[u2796].
It never fails.
A quiet transcript may be a slow agent, and a session waiting on its operator
writes nothing, so this is a floor, not a count.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-session " id" | path
The session to check: a session id, looked up in every folder under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ ,
or the path to a transcript
.I .jsonl
file.
Default:
.BR $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID .
With neither, the session is unknown and every check that needs its window
says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The repository to check.
Default: the current directory.
Any directory inside a repo will do; its top level is used.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output: the same content, with each check's status as
.BR pass ", " fail ", " warn " or " na ,
uncapped counts beside the capped lists, and
.B null
wherever a figure is unknown.
Fields are only ever added: the listings are
.BR askedAll ", " questions ", " otherRepos " and " liveSessions ,
and the verdict gains
.BR prompts ", " openQuestions ", " liveSessions ", " otherRepos ,
.BR otherRepoFail " and " otherRepoWarn ;
the verdict's
.B fail
includes a failing check in another repo.
.TP
.B \-\-identifiers
Print only the identifiers block that
.I RECORD.md
asks every session record and closing message to end with, and run none of the
checks.
See
.B IDENTIFIERS
below.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
the same fields as JSON.
Exit status is 0: the block is a record, not a verdict.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
A one-screen summary.
.SH IDENTIFIERS
A plain run prints this block after the verdict, so a session that runs
.B ccclose
at close has it in hand;
.B ccclose \-\-identifiers
prints the block alone.
It is a paste-ready markdown block, headed
.BR "## Identifiers" ,
with seven fields in the order
.I RECORD.md
gives them.
The fields are the same on every run: one that is empty prints
.BR none ,
and one that cannot be derived prints
.B by hand
and says why, never a guess.
.TP
.B Session
The session id, the models its transcript shows, and its window in UTC.
.TP
.B Commits
The commits the session made, as short shas, oldest first.
A commit is credited as fact only on evidence:
one
.B git commit
command of the session, or of one of its subagents, made it.
The command must fit the commit on four counts: its subject (a message's first
paragraph, its lines joined, as git reads it); the repo it ran in (its cwd, a
.B cd
or a
.B \-C
resolves into this repo or one of its worktrees); its result (not an error:
a command that failed for any reason counts for nothing); and the time (the
commit was made while the command ran).
Each command confirms at most one commit.
A commit command only quoted in a heredoc, a grep pattern or an echo runs
nothing and is no evidence.
This house commits with
.BR \-q ,
so the message is in the transcript but the sha is not;
.B \-m
and heredoc messages are read; so is a message read from a file with
.B \-F
or
.BR \-\-file ,
but only when the session itself wrote that file earlier, with its Write
tool or a heredoc, at the same path; a path holding a shell variable counts
only if the session assigned the variable a literal; a message in a variable,
or in a file nobody in the session wrote, cannot be matched), or
it is the squash merge of a PR the session ran
.B gh pr merge
on (a merge that did not fail).
A rebase, a
.B pull \-\-rebase
or an amend with no message of its own re-commits the session's own commits:
a copy made while one ran is credited if its subject is one the session gave a
commit command.
A worker dispatched as its own session has its own transcript, which is not
read: its commits print as unconfirmed.
When each credited commit's only parent is the one before it (an unbroken run
on one branch) the range follows, written
.IR first ^.. last
so that it is a git range over exactly those commits.
A commit from a session that overlapped breaks the run, so no range prints.
Two sub-lines follow, on every run.
.B unconfirmed
lists commits dated inside the window that no commit command of the session
matches by subject, repo, result and time: they may be a concurrent session's,
so check them before pasting.
A commit whose subject matches a command that does not fit says so on a line of
its own, as does a squash titled like a commit of the session, which may be its
own branch work merged by a peer.
A line says when subagent transcripts could not be read, since their commits
then print as unconfirmed.
.B left out
lists squash merges (a subject ending
.BR (#N) ,
or
.BR "Merge pull request #N" )
on the default branch's first-parent line whose PR the session ran no numbered
merge for.
It is a heuristic: a merge by another route (the web, auto-merge, no number) is
left out too, and so is a direct commit whose subject names an issue, so add
those by hand.
.TP
.B Pull requests
Every PR the session's own commands opened
.RB ( "gh pr create" ,
whose result names the PR) or ran a merge on
.RB ( "gh pr merge <n>" ),
joined with the open PRs ccclose credits to the window, each with its
state now from
.BR "gh pr view" .
A command that failed counts for nothing, and a PR whose URL names another
repository is counted and left to be listed by hand.
Without
.B gh
the numbers still print and the state reads unknown.
.TP
.B Board
Read from the commits' own diffs of the item store, one line per group:
.B completed
(an item whose first line became
.BR "\- [x]" ),
.B created
(an item file added),
.B "claimed (still open)"
(first line became
.BR "\- [~]" ,
and still is at the end of the window) and the
.B \[u23F3]
review items created (an item whose state line, the first line of its file,
gained a
.B \[u23F3]
outside a code span: the body of an item, or its close note, never counts).
These lines follow the credited commits only, and a claim counts as still open
only if nothing in the window, confirmed or not, moved the item since; a
confirmed claim that an unconfirmed commit closed or released has its own line.
A line,
.BR unconfirmed ,
lists what only the unconfirmed commits changed, to check before pasting.
A last line lists what is carried only by a squash merge the session did not
run, because the branch commits it repeats may be on no ref.
Items are named by section and item number, such as 180/060.
A hand-kept board has no item store to diff and prints by hand.
.TP
.B Worktrees and branches
Worktrees the session created, read from its
.B git worktree add
commands (the path, and the branch from
.B \-b
or the path's name) and its
.B EnterWorktree
results, because a worktree put away and its deleted branch leave no trace in
git.
Each prints whether the worktree is still present or put away, and whether the
branch is deleted or kept, merged or not.
A path with a shell variable is read from a literal assignment earlier in the
same command or the transcript; a path that cannot be resolved is left out, not
guessed.
An add inside a
.B for
loop over plain words (no glob, brace, variable or command substitution) is
listed once per word.
Adds left out are counted, and the block prints one line,
.RI \(lq N
worktree adds not resolved\(rq, so the list is known to be short.
Worktrees born inside the window and branches with a tip inside it are added.
.TP
.B Agents
How many subagents the session launched, how each ended (the same six counts,
failed included), and each one's short id and description.
The list is whole, not capped.
A sub-line,
.BR "other launched work" ,
gives the launched-work check's counts, zeros included, and each item, so the
field set stays seven.
.TP
.B Other repos
The repos read from the transcript (see
.BR "Other repos touched" ),
one line each with its commits from the window and the status of its checks
when not clean, the count of paths it could not place (fill in by hand), and a
note that a private repo is named generically in a public record.
With no transcript it prints
.BR "by hand" .
.PP
The last line is an italic note of how commits and board movement were
attributed.
.SH OUTPUT
The field set is the same on every run: every check prints, zeros print, and a
figure the tool could not read prints
.BR unknown ,
never 0.
Each listed set is capped at 25 lines (40 for the prompts), followed by the
number not shown.
The verdict comes before the identifiers block and is the line to quote, for
example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\[u274C] not ready to close \[u00B7] 2 fail \[u00B7] 1 unknown or partial
\[u00B7] 6 pass \[u00B7] 9 not applicable \[u00B7] 12 prompt(s) to answer
(3 close-shaped) \[u00B7] 1 unanswered question(s) \[u00B7] 2 other live
session(s) \[u00B7] 3 other repo(s) touched
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ * / id .jsonl
Session transcripts, read only and a line at a time.
.TP
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ * / id /subagents/agent\- * .jsonl
The session's subagent transcripts and their
.I .meta.json
files, read only.
.TP
.I scratchpadDirectory
The session's scratch folder, as the transcript records it; listed, never
changed.
.TP
.I docs/sessions/ \fRor\fP sessions/
The session log, one file per session.
.TP
.I docs/SESSIONS.md \fRor\fP SESSIONS.md
The retired one-file session index, read only while a repo still keeps it.
.TP
.I docs/ROADMAP.md \fRor\fP ROADMAP.md
The board index, with its
.I roadmap/
item store when the board is generated.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
The default session, set by Claude Code in every session's shell.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_PS
The
.BR ps (1)
executable the launched-work check reads the process table with, called as
.B "ps \-Eww \-axo pid=,ppid=,etime=,command=" .
Default:
.BR ps .
The tests point it at a stub, so none reads the real table.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_GH
The
.B gh
executable to run.
Default:
.BR gh .
The tests point it at a stub.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_ATELIER
The atelier checkout the pin check measures against, and whose
.I tools/pins.py
it runs.
If set it is used as given: a wrong value is "no result", not a fall-back.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_PINS_TIMEOUT
The pin check's time limit, in seconds.
Default: 20.
.TP
.B ATELIER_ESTATE_ROOT
Where the pin check looks for an
.I atelier
checkout when it has no better answer.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the current session's close, from the repo it worked in:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check an earlier session, against another checkout:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose \-\-session 1edeadf7\-7675\-488a\-a722\-a97ab5098531 \-\-root ~/src/demo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Feed the result to another tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose \-\-json | jq '.checks[] | select(.status == "fail")'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the identifiers block, to paste at the tail of a session record:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose \-\-identifiers
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No check failed.
Some may warn or be unknown; the verdict line says so.
.B \-\-identifiers
always exits 0.
.TP
.B 1
At least one check failed.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown flag, a flag with no value, or a
.B \-\-root
that is not a directory.
.SH NOTES
.B ccclose
is read-only.
It never commits, pushes, removes a worktree or edits a file in the repo it
checks, and fetches nothing there.
The one exception is the pin check, which fetches in the \fIatelier\fP checkout
(see that check).
Every git call runs with
.BR \-\-no\-optional\-locks ,
because a plain
.B git status
refreshes the index under
.IR index.lock ,
and on a checkout another session shares, that write can collide with its
commit.
Its only network use is
.BR gh ,
and only when
.B gh
is installed and
.B gh auth status
succeeds; otherwise the pull request and floor checks are unknown.
The pin check also needs network for pins.py's fetch, and says "no result"
when it cannot.
.PP
It does not check whether the session's findings (defects, learnings,
evidence, assumptions) reached the record.
That is a reading of meaning, not a fact a fresh read can prove; the session
record check proves a record was written, and the asks list puts the rest to
the session.
.PP
The transcript format is internal to Claude Code and can shift between
releases; the classifiers are cctranscript's, so a fix there reaches both.
The design of record is
.IR instruments/ccclose.design.md .
